#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Start the installer. The one way in on this image — the systemd unit on tty1
# runs this, and so does anybody typing `installer` at a prompt after leaving
# it. One entry point, so both ways behave identically.
#
# Three things it settles, and they are the reason it exists rather than a
# symlink:
#
# The console is dressed the moment before the interface draws on it. The font
# decides which glyphs exist at all and the palette decides what the sixteen
# colour slots look like — see installer-console-theme. This is done here rather
# than in the unit because the unit's ExecStartPre runs while the boot splash is
# still holding the console: setfont finds no console to load into and gives up,
# which as an ExecStartPre is a failure nobody ever sees. By the time this runs,
# the console has been handed over. It is also why somebody who leaves the
# installer and types `installer` again gets the same console back, which a unit
# could never do. Neither is worth refusing to start over, so both may fail —
# loudly, into the journal, rather than silently.
#
# The runtime looks for its installer.yaml beside its own binary, so it is
# started out of the folder it was installed into.
#
# And its answers land beside that tree: installer.conf and installer.log go to
# the working directory, so leaving the installer and starting it again from any
# prompt, in any directory, picks up exactly where it left off. A second copy of
# the answers in ~ would be a machine that quietly forgot what it was told.
set -eu

setfont Lat2-Terminus16 || true
installer-console-theme || true

cd /opt/installer
exec ./installer "$@"
